(ns kotoba.phone.lifecycle
  "MSISDN lifecycle as a pure state machine.

  This namespace exists so that the question -- is this number operation even
  reachable from the state we have on record? -- is answered by a total,
  deterministic function with no I/O, no model and no policy. It is the intended
  callee of a consent surface's pre-check (ADR-2607300300 D2), exactly as
  kotoba.esim.lifecycle is for profiles and kotoba.card.lifecycle is for cards,
  and it is deliberately the same shape as both so a reader who knows one knows
  all three.

  What this namespace decides: structural reachability, the one-active-MSISDN-
  per-profile invariant, terminality, and the recycling path. What it
  deliberately does NOT decide: whether an operator holds the numbering block,
  whether a port-out is fraudulent, whether a jurisdiction permits an
  assignment. Those need judgement or law and belong to the actor's Governor.

  ## Recycling is enforced by the SHAPE of the graph, not by a check

  A released number carries the previous subject's account-recovery reach: SMS
  one-time codes, 'forgot password' callbacks, and the second factor for
  everything they signed up to. Re-allocating it early hands all of that to
  whoever gets it next, and that is the single worst thing a numbering plane can
  do quietly.

  So `:assign` is NOT reachable from `:released`. The only path back is
  `:release -> :quarantine -> :recycle -> :reserve -> :assign`, and `:recycle`
  refuses unless the caller states, as a fact, that the aging window has
  elapsed. A caller who wants to skip the wait cannot express it: there is no
  transition that would let them, and an absent fact refuses rather than
  passing. The window's LENGTH is policy and lives in kotoba.phone.numbering;
  what lives here is that it cannot be routed around.

  Portable (.cljc) across JVM / ClojureScript / SCI / GraalVM."
    (:require [clojure.string :as str])
)

(def states
  "MSISDN states from the numbering operator's point of view.

  `:foreign` is this library's modeling of a number that exists in the world but
  not on this operator's plane -- it is what makes port-in expressible as a
  transition rather than a special case, the same convenience
  kotoba.esim.lifecycle's `:absent` is for a profile not yet installed.

  `:released` and `:quarantined` are two states rather than one because they
  answer different questions: `:released` means the subject is gone,
  `:quarantined` means the number is aging and MAY NOT be re-allocated yet. One
  state could not say both, and collapsing them is how an operator ends up
  re-issuing a number the week its previous owner stopped paying."
  #{:foreign :unallocated :reserved :assigned :active :suspended
    :porting-out :released :quarantined :ported-out})

(def terminal-states
  "States no operation leads out of. A completed port-out is terminal FOR THIS
  OPERATOR: the number continues to exist, on the recipient's plane, and nothing
  this operator records about it afterwards is authoritative."
  #{:ported-out})

(def operations
  "Number operations, each declaring the states it is reachable from and the
  state it lands in.

  Three rules here are easy to guess wrong:

  - `:activate` is legal only from `:assigned`. Bringing a SUSPENDED line back
    is `:restore`, a separately named decision, for the same reason
    kotoba.card.lifecycle refuses to call unblocking an activate: a recovery and
    a first activation carry different risk and should not share an audit word.

  - `:release` is not reachable from `:porting-out`. A port-out in flight must
    complete or be cancelled first; releasing underneath it would leave the
    recipient importing a number this operator has already put back in the pool.

  - `:port-in` lands in `:assigned`, not `:active`. The cutover is its own
    decision and gets its own record, which is also the point at which the
    one-active-MSISDN invariant below is tested."
  {:port-in           {:from #{:foreign}      :to :assigned}
   :reserve           {:from #{:unallocated}  :to :reserved}
   :assign            {:from #{:reserved}     :to :assigned}
   :activate          {:from #{:assigned}     :to :active}
   :suspend           {:from #{:active}       :to :suspended}
   :restore           {:from #{:suspended}    :to :active}
   :release           {:from #{:assigned :active :suspended} :to :released}
   :quarantine        {:from #{:released}     :to :quarantined}
   :recycle           {:from #{:quarantined}  :to :unallocated}
   :port-out-request  {:from #{:active}       :to :porting-out}
   :port-out-cancel   {:from #{:porting-out}  :to :active}
   :port-out-complete {:from #{:porting-out}  :to :ported-out}})

(def takeover-signals
  "Operations that move a line OUT of the current subject's control.

  This is the vocabulary the cross-domain posture reads (ADR-2607300300 D4).
  Whoever takes over a phone number has taken over the second factor for
  everything else, so these are the number-plane analogue of an eSIM ownership
  transfer and must lower the same subject's card posture. It lives in this
  library rather than in a consent surface so that every consumer reads ONE
  definition of 'the line changed hands'.

  `:port-out-request` counts, not just completion: the request is the signal.
  Waiting for a port-out to succeed before restricting spend hands the attacker
  exactly the window the restriction exists to close."
  #{:port-out-request :port-out-complete :release})

(def provenance-unsettled-signals
  "Operations after which the line's history is FRESH, which is a different
  worry from a takeover and points the other way.

  A number that has just been ported in, assigned, or recycled may be a stolen
  line, or may still be receiving the previous holder's account-recovery
  traffic. It should not immediately be treated as a settled second factor. A
  consumer that only reads `takeover-signals` sees the victim's side and misses
  the attacker's."
  #{:port-in :assign :recycle})

(defn terminal?
  "True when state admits no further operation."
  [state]
  (contains? terminal-states state))

(defn reachable?
  "True when operation is admissible from state. False for any unknown state or
  operation -- an unrecognized input is never treated as permissive."
  [state operation]
  (boolean
   (when-let [{:keys [from]} (get operations operation)]
     (and (contains? states state)
          (contains? from state)))))

(defn next-state
  "The state operation lands in when applied to state, or nil when the operation
  is not reachable from it."
  [state operation]
  (when (reachable? state operation)
    (get-in operations [operation :to])))

(defn transition-issues
  "Return a seq of reasons operation cannot be applied to state, empty when it
  can. Reasons are data so a governor can cite one rather than re-derive it."
  [state operation]
  (cond
    (not (contains? operations operation))
    [{:phone/issue :operation/unknown :phone/operation operation}]

    (not (contains? states state))
    [{:phone/issue :state/unknown :phone/state state}]

    (terminal? state)
    [{:phone/issue :state/terminal :phone/state state :phone/operation operation}]

    (not (reachable? state operation))
    [{:phone/issue     :transition/unreachable
      :phone/state     state
      :phone/operation operation
      :phone/from      (get-in operations [operation :from] #{})}]

    :else []))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The one-active-MSISDN-per-profile invariant
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn active-msisdns
  "The MSISDNs of records currently in `:active` state. records is a collection
  of number records (see kotoba.phone.numbering/record)."
  [records]
  (into #{} (comp (filter #(= :active (:phone/state %)))
                  (map :phone/msisdn))
        records))

(defn binding-conflict
  "Return the MSISDN already active on this record's ICCID, or nil when nothing
  is.

  A profile carries one subscriber number at a time, so activating a second
  MSISDN against the same ICCID takes the first line away as a side effect --
  the same shape as kotoba.esim.lifecycle/enabled-conflict, and surfaced for the
  same reason: the incumbent's loss should be a decision somebody made, not a
  consequence they discover.

  A record with no ICCID conflicts with nothing. A number can be held and billed
  without being bound to a profile, and refusing that would make the pre-check
  stricter than the world."
  [records msisdn iccid]
  (when (some? iccid)
    (->> records
         (filter #(and (= :active (:phone/state %))
                       (= iccid (:phone/iccid %))
                       (not= msisdn (:phone/msisdn %))))
         (map :phone/msisdn)
         first)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Applying an operation
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- recycle-issues
  "Why `:recycle` may not run. The aging window is passed IN as a fact rather
  than computed here, because this namespace has no clock and must not acquire
  one: a state machine whose answer depends on when you ask it cannot be
  re-checked by a governor against the same recorded state.

  An absent fact refuses. `nil` is not 'probably fine' -- it is nobody having
  established that the window elapsed, and re-allocating on that basis is
  precisely the failure this transition exists to prevent."
  [{:phone/keys [quarantine-elapsed?]}]
  (when-not (true? quarantine-elapsed?)
    [{:phone/issue :recycle/quarantine-incomplete
      :phone/detail
      "quarantine が明けたという事実が示されていません（kotoba.phone.numbering/quarantine-elapsed? の結果を渡すこと）"}]))

(defn- assign-issues
  "Why `:assign` may not run. A number assigned to nobody is a number whose
  subsequent operations have no owner to consent for them."
  [{:phone/keys [subject]}]
  (when (or (nil? subject) (and (string? subject) (str/blank? subject)))
    [{:phone/issue :assign/subject-missing}]))

(defn apply-event
  "Apply operation to the number identified by msisdn within records, returning
  either

    {:phone/ok? true  :phone/records <updated> :phone/from s :phone/to s'}

  or

    {:phone/ok? false :phone/issues [...]}

  and never throwing.

  `opts` carries the facts this machine refuses to fetch:

    :phone/quarantine-elapsed?  required by :recycle
    :phone/subject              required by :assign and :port-in
    :phone/iccid                the profile the number is being bound to

  This is the whole surface a pre-check needs: it is total, it returns data, and
  its refusals carry a reason."
  ([records msisdn operation] (apply-event records msisdn operation {}))
  ([records msisdn operation opts]
   (let [target (first (filter #(= msisdn (:phone/msisdn %)) records))
         state  (:phone/state target)
         iccid  (or (:phone/iccid opts) (:phone/iccid target))
         issues (cond
                  (nil? target)
                  [{:phone/issue :number/not-found :phone/msisdn msisdn}]

                  (seq (transition-issues state operation))
                  (vec (transition-issues state operation))

                  (= :recycle operation) (recycle-issues opts)

                  (contains? #{:assign :port-in} operation) (assign-issues opts)

                  :else nil)
         issues (or issues
                    (when (and (contains? #{:activate :restore} operation)
                               (binding-conflict records msisdn iccid))
                      [{:phone/issue     :activate/would-displace
                        :phone/msisdn    msisdn
                        :phone/iccid     iccid
                        :phone/incumbent (binding-conflict records msisdn iccid)}]))]
     (if (seq issues)
       {:phone/ok? false :phone/issues (vec issues)}
       (let [to (next-state state operation)]
         {:phone/ok?      true
          :phone/from     state
          :phone/to       to
          :phone/records  (mapv (fn [r]
                                  (if (= msisdn (:phone/msisdn r))
                                    (cond-> (assoc r :phone/state to)
                                      (:phone/subject opts) (assoc :phone/subject (:phone/subject opts))
                                      (some? (:phone/iccid opts)) (assoc :phone/iccid (:phone/iccid opts))
                                      ;; A recycled number keeps no trace of who
                                      ;; held it. Carrying the subject forward
                                      ;; into the pool is how a 'previous owner'
                                      ;; field ends up on a stranger's line.
                                      (= :recycle operation) (assoc :phone/subject nil :phone/iccid nil))
                                    r))
                                records)})))))

(defn describe
  "A one-line human-readable rendering of a transition, for an operator log.
  Pure string building; no formatting library."
  [state operation]
  (if-let [to (next-state state operation)]
    (str (name operation) ": " (name state) " -> " (name to))
    (str (name operation) ": refused from " (if (keyword? state) (name state) (str state))
         " (reachable from "
         (str/join ", " (sort (map name (get-in operations [operation :from] #{}))))
         ")")))
